LeetCode 100 — Same Tree

Problem: Given the roots of two binary trees p and q, determine whether they are structurally identical and have the same node values.

Two trees are the same if:

Corresponding nodes have the same value.
Corresponding left subtrees are the same.
Corresponding right subtrees are the same.
Example
Tree p:        1
              / \
             2   3

Tree q:        1
              / \
             2   3

Output:

true

But:

Tree p:        1
              /
             2

Tree q:        1
                \
                 2

Output:

false

Even though the values are the same, the structure is different.

Approach 1: Brute Force — Traversal + Comparison
Idea

We can traverse both trees and store their complete structure and values in arrays/lists.

A simple way is to perform preorder traversal including null markers.

Why do we need null markers?

Consider:

Tree 1:    1          Tree 2:    1
           /                     \
          2                       2

Normal preorder gives:

[1, 2]
[1, 2]

They look identical, even though the structures differ.

With null markers:

Tree 1 → [1, 2, null, null, null]
Tree 2 → [1, null, 2, null, null]

Now they are different.

Code
class Solution {

    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        List<Integer> list1 = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> list2 = new ArrayList<>();

        preorder(p, list1);
        preorder(q, list2);

        return list1.equals(list2);
    }

    public void preorder(TreeNode root, List<Integer> list) {
        if (root == null) {
            list.add(null);
            return;
        }

        list.add(root.val);

        preorder(root.left, list);
        preorder(root.right, list);
    }
}

