If there are n nodes:

Time: O(n)
Space: O(n) for storing traversal results
Recursion stack: O(h)

This works, but we are storing the entire trees even though we only need to know whether they match.

Approach 2: Iterative BFS Using Queue
Idea

We can compare the trees level by level.

Put both roots into queues.

At every step:

Remove one node from each tree.
If both are null, continue.
If only one is null, trees are different.
If values differ, trees are different.
Add their left children.
Add their right children.

Code
class Solution {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();

        queue.offer(p);
        queue.offer(q);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {

            TreeNode first = queue.poll();
            TreeNode second = queue.poll();

            // Both are null
            if (first == null && second == null) {
                continue;
            }

            // One is null
            if (first == null || second == null) {
                return false;
            }

            // Different values
            if (first.val != second.val) {
                return false;
            }

            queue.offer(first.left);
            queue.offer(second.left);

            queue.offer(first.right);
            queue.offer(second.right);
        }

        return true;
    }
}

