Approach 3: Recursive DFS — Optimal and Most Natural

This is the cleanest solution for this problem.

Core Idea

At every pair of nodes (p, q), there are only a few possibilities.

Case 1: Both are null
p = null
q = null

There is nothing to compare.

Therefore:

return true;
Case 2: One is null

For example:

p = 2
q = null

One tree has a node while the other doesn't.

Therefore:

return false;
Case 3: Values are different
p.val = 2
q.val = 5

Therefore:

return false;
Case 4: Values match

If:

p.val == q.val

we still need to compare:

p.left  with q.left
p.right with q.right

So:

return p.val == q.val
       && isSameTree(p.left, q.left)
       && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);

Your Code — Optimal Solution

class Solution {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        if(p==null&&q==null) return true;
        if(p==null||q==null) return false;
        return p.val==q.val && isSameTree(p.left, q.left) && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);
    }
}

Your solution is already optimal in time complexity.

