Why && Makes the Code Efficient

Your return statement:

return p.val==q.val 
       && isSameTree(p.left, q.left) 
       && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);

uses short-circuit evaluation.

Suppose:

p =       1
         /
        2

q =       1
         /
        5

At node 1:

1 == 1 → true

Then compare left:

2 == 5 → false

Because the second condition is already false, Java doesn't need to evaluate the remaining condition.

So it can stop immediately.

This is useful because if the trees differ near the root, we don't need to traverse the entire tree.

Approach Comparison
Approach	Idea	Time	Extra Space
Brute Force	Store preorder + null markers	O(n)	O(n)
BFS	Compare level by level	O(n)	O(n) worst case
Recursive DFS	Compare corresponding nodes directly	O(n)	O(h)

Where:

n = number of nodes
h = height of the tree

For a balanced tree:

h = O(log n)

For a completely skewed tree:

h = O(n)
Why Recursive DFS Is the Best Approach Here

The problem itself is recursive:

Are two trees the same?

To answer that, we ask:

Are their roots the same?
        +
Are their left subtrees the same?
        +
Are their right subtrees the same?

That directly translates to:

p.val == q.val
&& isSameTree(p.left, q.left)
&& isSameTree(p.right, q.right)

So recursion matches the structure of the problem perfectly.

Important Edge Cases
1. Both trees empty
p = null
q = null

Result:

true
2. One tree empty
p = 1
q = null

Result:

false
3. Single nodes with same value
p = 1
q = 1

Result:

true
4. Single nodes with different values
p = 1
q = 2

Result:

false
5. Same values but different structure
    1              1
   /                \
  2                  2

Result:

false
The Pattern to Remember

For many binary-tree comparison problems, use this template:

if (p == null && q == null)
    return true;

if (p == null || q == null)
    return false;

if (p.val != q.val)
    return false;

return isSameTree(p.left, q.left)
    && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);

Or your compact version:

if(p==null&&q==null) return true;
if(p==null||q==null) return false;

return p.val==q.val
    && isSameTree(p.left,q.left)
    && isSameTree(p.right,q.right);
🧠 Interview Pattern

Tree comparison = compare corresponding nodes.

Always think:

1. Both null?       → true
2. One null?        → false
3. Values differ?   → false
4. Check left
5. Check right

This same recursive pattern appears in problems involving tree equality, subtree checking, tree symmetry, tree cloning/comparison, and structural validation.
